# governance/allowed_signers — the public keys a vote-record `signature` is verified against (#3927 item 4).
#
# OpenSSH allowed_signers format (ssh-keygen(1), "ALLOWED SIGNERS"): principal, ONE comma-joined options
# token, key type, key. `namespaces` restricts the key to vote-record signatures (a git or file
# signature by the same key cannot be replayed as a ratification). The validity window is how a key
# is rotated: add `valid-before` to the old line rather than deleting it, and add the new key on its
# own line. Rotation keeps every record the old key signed verifiable, because the verifier anchors
# the window check at the record's `recordedAt` (`ssh-keygen -Overify-time`), not at the wall clock;
# deleting the line instead would turn those records into `unknown-signer`. Dates without a `Z`
# suffix are read in the verifier's local zone — write windows as `YYYYMMDD[Z]`, UTC. Governor path:
# a change here needs ratification, like the ledger it vouches for.
#
# What a listed key proves when it signs: access to the private key from the environment that ran
# the append — not a human's presence. See docs/security/audit-hash-chain-threat-model.md and #6257.
#
# Two kinds of principal (#6257 panel 2026-09-15, option B): `nexus-agent@<host>` is a key the
# autonomous loop signs with — generated by scripts/vote-record-keygen.ts OUTSIDE any checkout, at
# ~/.nexus-agents/auth/vote-record-signing.key, no passphrase, and picked up by
# append-ratification-record.ts by default. Any other principal is a human-held key, reserved for
# records a human appended (`--as-owner`). The gate prints `signed:agent` / `signed:owner` from the
# prefix. This is ATTRIBUTION of which process appended, not host isolation: both keys live on the
# operator's host and the agent process can read both.
